const CHAPTERS = [
{n:1,name:'अर्जुनविषादयोग',title:'Arjuna’s dilemma',count:47,theme:'When your values collide',summary:'On the battlefield, Arjuna asks Krishna to place their chariot between the armies. Seeing teachers, friends and relatives, he questions the purpose and cost of victory. His bow slips and he refuses to fight. The teaching begins with an unresolved moral crisis.',example:'Before accepting a role that conflicts with your values, name the people affected and the competing duties. Clarity begins by admitting the conflict.',question:'What exactly feels in conflict: fear, loyalty, responsibility, or all three?'},
{n:2,name:'सांख्ययोग',title:'Wisdom & steady action',count:72,theme:'Act with care; loosen your grip',summary:'Arjuna asks for instruction. Krishna teaches about the enduring Self, duty, disciplined action without attachment to its fruits, and the qualities of steady wisdom. The chapter moves from grief toward discernment and self-mastery.',example:'Prepare seriously for an exam, then separate the quality of your effort from the score you cannot fully control. This is an application of the chapter, not a promise of success.',question:'What is the next responsible action available to you?'},
{n:3,name:'कर्मयोग',title:'The yoga of action',count:43,theme:'Responsibility without ego',summary:'Krishna explains why simply avoiding action is not freedom. Action offered without selfish attachment, concern for the welfare of the world, and restraint of desire are central. The example set by a leader affects others.',example:'In a group project, complete your share even when praise is uneven. Discuss unfairness constructively without abandoning your responsibility.',question:'Who is affected by how you choose to act?'},
{n:4,name:'ज्ञानकर्मसंन्यासयोग',title:'Knowledge & action',count:42,theme:'Learn before you assume',summary:'Krishna describes the transmission of yoga, his manifestation when dharma declines, and the subtle relationship of action, inaction and knowledge. Different forms of sacrifice culminate in the importance of insight, inquiry and learning.',example:'Ask an experienced mentor a precise question, listen carefully, and test your understanding through practice instead of collecting impressive quotations.',question:'Which assumption needs to be examined?'},
{n:5,name:'कर्मसंन्यासयोग',title:'Renunciation in action',count:29,theme:'Work without inner possession',summary:'Renunciation and disciplined action are related paths. Krishna describes acting without possessiveness, seeing beings with equality, and finding peace beyond attachment and aversion. Outer activity need not prevent inner freedom.',example:'Give a classmate useful help without keeping a mental debt ledger. Equal regard also means treating people respectfully when they cannot help your status.',question:'Where does “my achievement” become possessiveness?'},
{n:6,name:'ध्यानयोग',title:'The discipline of meditation',count:47,theme:'Return, gently and repeatedly',summary:'Krishna teaches self-discipline, moderation, meditation, and repeatedly returning a wandering mind to the Self. Arjuna raises the difficulty of controlling the mind and the fate of an unsuccessful practitioner. Practice and detachment are emphasized.',example:'When studying, notice distraction and return to one chosen task. This borrows the discipline of returning attention; the verse’s original setting is spiritual meditation.',question:'What would returning look like, instead of giving up?'},
{n:7,name:'ज्ञानविज्ञानयोग',title:'Knowledge & realization',count:30,theme:'Look beyond appearances',summary:'Krishna describes his relation to material and conscious existence, the difficulty of crossing maya, and different motives that bring people to devotion. Knowledge deepens toward recognizing the divine as the ground of all.',example:'Notice how gratitude changes when ordinary water, light and life are approached with reverence. This is a devotional reflection, not a scientific claim.',question:'What ordinary part of life have you stopped noticing?'},
{n:8,name:'अक्षरब्रह्मयोग',title:'The imperishable Brahman',count:28,theme:'What you repeatedly remember',summary:'Arjuna asks about Brahman, the self, action and divine presence. Krishna teaches remembrance at the time of death and throughout life, and describes cosmological cycles and spiritual destinations.',example:'Consider what your everyday attention is training you to remember. Set aside a short, regular period for a value or prayer that matters to you.',question:'What do you want your daily attention to be shaped by?'},
{n:9,name:'राजविद्याराजगुह्ययोग',title:'The royal knowledge',count:34,theme:'Sincerity in small offerings',summary:'Krishna presents a profound teaching of divine immanence and transcendence, devotion, and offering one’s actions. A simple offering made with devotion matters; spiritual turning is not restricted to social prestige.',example:'A small, sincere act of service can be meaningful even without public recognition or an expensive offering.',question:'What could you offer today with sincerity?'},
{n:10,name:'विभूतियोग',title:'Divine manifestations',count:42,theme:'Recognize excellence with humility',summary:'Krishna describes divine manifestations through many examples of beings, qualities and powers. These examples direct Arjuna toward recognizing the divine source of splendor and excellence.',example:'Appreciate another person’s excellence without making it a verdict against yourself. Let admiration lead to learning and gratitude.',question:'What can you admire without needing to possess it?'},
{n:11,name:'विश्वरूपदर्शनयोग',title:'The universal form',count:55,theme:'Humility before the whole',summary:'Given divine sight, Arjuna beholds Krishna’s universal form, both wondrous and terrifying. The vision of time and destruction transforms Arjuna’s understanding; he then asks to see Krishna’s familiar form again.',example:'A decision can have consequences beyond the part you see. Seek wider context and remain humble about your own perspective. The cosmic vision belongs to the sacred narrative.',question:'What larger context is missing from your view?'},
{n:12,name:'भक्तियोग',title:'The yoga of devotion',count:20,theme:'Let devotion shape your conduct',summary:'Krishna discusses devotion to the personal divine and the unmanifest, offers graded practices, and describes a beloved devotee through qualities such as friendliness, compassion, freedom from possessiveness and steadiness.',example:'An everyday expression of devotion can be listening without contempt, keeping a promise, or helping without demanding credit.',question:'Can your devotion be noticed in how you treat someone?'},
{n:13,name:'क्षेत्रक्षेत्रज्ञविभागयोग',title:'The field & its knower',count:35,theme:'Observe what changes',summary:'The body is described as the field and awareness as its knower. Krishna discusses knowledge, what is to be known, and the relation of prakriti and purusha. Humility and non-injury appear among the qualities of knowledge.',example:'Notice “anger is present” without reducing your entire identity to “I am an angry person.” This is a modern reflection, not a full account of the chapter’s metaphysics.',question:'What can you observe without immediately identifying with it?'},
{n:14,name:'गुणत्रयविभागयोग',title:'The three gunas',count:27,theme:'Notice the quality of your actions',summary:'Sattva, rajas and tamas are described as qualities of prakriti that bind embodied beings in different ways. Krishna describes their effects and the person who goes beyond them through steadiness and devotion.',example:'Reflect on clarity, restless striving and inertia in your day. The gunas are a traditional philosophical framework, not a medical or personality diagnosis.',question:'Is this choice coming from clarity, agitation, or avoidance?'},
{n:15,name:'पुरुषोत्तमयोग',title:'The supreme person',count:20,theme:'Find what is worth holding to',summary:'An inverted tree represents conditioned existence. Krishna teaches detachment, the living being’s relation to the divine, and the distinction between the perishable, the imperishable and the Supreme Person.',example:'Question a status goal you have inherited without examination. Ask what is enduringly meaningful before investing all your energy in it.',question:'Which attachment is deciding your direction?'},
{n:16,name:'दैवासुरसम्पद्विभागयोग',title:'Qualities that shape a life',count:24,theme:'Character is practiced',summary:'Krishna contrasts divine and demonic dispositions. Fearlessness, self-restraint, truthfulness and compassion stand against arrogance, anger and cruelty. Desire, anger and greed are identified as destructive gateways.',example:'When you want to win an argument at any cost, check whether your words remain truthful and kind. A difficult conversation can still have boundaries.',question:'Which quality do you want to practice in the next conversation?'},
{n:17,name:'श्रद्धात्रयविभागयोग',title:'Faith, speech & intention',count:28,theme:'How you do something matters',summary:'Faith, food, sacrifice, austerity and giving are examined through the three gunas. Disciplines of body, speech and mind emphasize sincerity and intention. The chapter concludes with Om Tat Sat.',example:'Before speaking, ask whether your words are truthful, useful and considerate. Being considerate does not require concealing a necessary truth.',question:'What is the intention beneath your action?'},
{n:18,name:'मोक्षसंन्यासयोग',title:'Freedom & surrender',count:78,theme:'Reflect, choose, and act',summary:'Krishna revisits renunciation, action, knowledge, duty, the gunas and devotion. He invites Arjuna to reflect fully, then offers the teaching of surrender. Arjuna’s confusion is dispelled and he chooses to act.',example:'After seeking counsel, take responsibility for a considered decision. Spiritual surrender need not mean refusing to think or making someone else choose for you.',question:'Having reflected, what will you now do?'}
];
const REFLECTIONS={
'1.28':['Name the distress','Arjuna sees his own people and describes his distress. The dialogue does not begin by pretending the problem is easy.','Before a difficult conversation, write down what you are afraid of losing.','What is making this situation difficult for you?'],
'1.47':['Pause before the teaching','Arjuna puts down his bow, overwhelmed by grief. This is a moment in the story, not a command for every reader to withdraw from duty.','If you feel flooded during an argument, ask for a short pause and agree when to return.','What support would help you resume the conversation?'],
'2.7':['Make room to learn','Arjuna admits confusion about dharma and asks Krishna to teach him. Recognizing that you need guidance is part of the dialogue.','Tell a mentor: “I see two responsibilities pulling me apart. Can you help me think through them?”','Who can help you think without making the decision for you?'],
'2.14':['A feeling is not the whole horizon','Krishna describes sense-born pleasure and pain as coming and going and calls for endurance. This does not mean ignoring danger or accepting mistreatment.','A disappointing score feels intense today. Give the feeling space while deciding how to revise your preparation.','What is temporary here, and what still needs action?'],
'2.20':['The teaching of the enduring Self','Krishna teaches that the Self is unborn and undying. This is a spiritual teaching about atman, not an empirically proven account of death.','During change, reflect on whether possessions and roles exhaust who you are.','Which roles have you mistaken for your entire identity?'],
'2.47':['Your effort is yours to offer','Krishna joins responsibility for action with non-attachment to its fruits, and explicitly rejects attachment to inaction. Non-attachment is not carelessness about consequences.','You apply for an internship. Work carefully on your portfolio and application; let the response inform your next step without making it the measure of your worth.','What can you do well today, even without a guaranteed result?'],
'2.48':['Steadiness in success and failure','Krishna asks Arjuna to act with equanimity, relinquishing attachment. Steadiness makes room for responsible action; it does not demand emotional numbness.','After an interview, review what you can improve without either declaring yourself superior or writing yourself off.','Can you learn from the outcome without becoming the outcome?'],
'2.62':['Notice the beginning of the chain','The verse traces a progression from dwelling on sense-objects to attachment, desire and anger. It describes an escalating pattern to notice.','Repeatedly checking someone’s achievements can turn admiration into comparison and resentment. Close the feed and name the desire underneath.','What are you repeatedly feeding your attention?'],
'2.63':['Interrupt before judgment is lost','The next verse carries the chain from anger toward confusion and the loss of discernment. Read it alongside 2.62.','Do not send the first hostile message you draft. Return when you can state the problem without trying to hurt the person.','What would help protect your judgment right now?'],
'2.64':['Restraint without hostility','Krishna describes moving among sense-objects with disciplined senses, free from attachment and aversion, leading toward serenity.','A phone can remain a useful tool when you decide when and why to use it. You do not need to hate it to set a boundary.','What boundary would let you use this responsibly?'],
'3.8':['Begin with the work before you','Krishna says to perform the action that ought to be done; action is preferable to inaction. The wider discussion concerns duty and liberation.','Open the assignment and complete one defined part before redesigning your entire routine.','What is one small, necessary action you can finish?'],
'3.19':['Care without clinging','Necessary action is to be done without attachment. This is a discipline of intention, not permission to disregard those affected by your work.','Help organize a college event well even when someone else will present it on stage.','Can the work be worthwhile without applause?'],
'3.21':['Your example travels','Others follow the standards set by an influential person. Leadership therefore includes the conduct people see, not just instructions they hear.','If you lead a team, be punctual yourself before asking everyone else to be punctual.','Which behavior are you making normal around you?'],
'3.30':['Offer the action','Krishna calls for offering actions to him while relinquishing possessiveness and feverish anxiety. The verse is devotional and addressed to Arjuna’s duty.','Before beginning important work, dedicate it to something larger than recognition. Then give it full attention.','What purpose beyond self-display could guide this work?'],
'4.34':['Ask with humility','Learning is connected with reverence, sincere questioning and service to those who see the truth. Inquiry is part of the relationship.','Bring a specific question and a worked example to your teacher instead of pretending you understand.','What is the question you have been avoiding?'],
'5.18':['Equal regard','The wise are described as seeing with equality across socially and physically different beings. The verse points to spiritual vision beyond outward distinctions.','Offer the same basic courtesy to a cleaner, a professor and a classmate.','Whose dignity have you overlooked?'],
'6.5':['Become an ally to yourself','The self can function as friend or enemy in the work of self-uplift. Read this as a call to cultivate discipline, not as blame for suffering or illness.','Replace “I always fail” with a concrete review: what happened, and what support or change would help tomorrow?','What would a fair inner voice say?'],
'6.17':['Make the practice sustainable','Moderation in food, recreation, effort, sleep and waking supports yoga. The verse does not prescribe one exact schedule for every person.','Choose a study routine that leaves room for regular rest instead of an impossible all-night plan.','Which part of your routine has become extreme?'],
'6.26':['The practice is the return','Whenever the mind wanders, the practitioner brings it back to the Self. The original context is meditation; returning to a task is a practical analogy.','You notice you have been scrolling while studying. Put the phone away and return to the next line, without wasting another ten minutes scolding yourself.','Where will you return your attention now?'],
'6.35':['Practice, and let go','Krishna agrees that the mind is difficult to restrain, then points to practice and detachment. Difficulty does not invalidate the practice.','Repeat a manageable habit after a missed day. Let go of needing a flawless record before you continue.','What is small enough to repeat tomorrow?'],
'6.40':['Effort is not simply erased','Krishna reassures Arjuna about the spiritual practitioner who falls short. The assurance belongs to a teaching about the path of yoga.','As an analogy, a setback does not erase what you have learned. Return to the part of the practice you can sustain.','What have you already learned that remains useful?'],
'9.26':['Sincerity over display','Krishna accepts a simple offering made with devotion. The emphasis is on devotion, not the market value of what is offered.','Give a sincere few minutes of attention to someone instead of making an impressive promise you cannot keep.','What small offering can you make wholeheartedly?'],
'9.27':['Bring intention to ordinary actions','Krishna asks that actions, offerings, gifts and austerities be offered to him. Devotion enters ordinary life.','Before a routine task, choose to do it with care rather than resentment.','How could this ordinary action become an offering?'],
'12.13':['Compassion as a way of being','The devotee is described as free from hatred, friendly and compassionate, without possessiveness and ego, and steady in pleasure and pain.','Listen to a friend’s complaint before preparing your defense. You can be compassionate and still set a boundary.','Can you understand their pain without surrendering your judgment?'],
'12.14':['Devotion with steadiness','Contentment, disciplined resolve and dedication of mind and intellect continue the portrait begun in 12.13.','Keep a small commitment even when the mood that inspired it has passed.','What does steady care look like today?'],
'16.1':['Cultivate a disposition','Fearlessness, purity of mind, knowledge, giving and self-restraint begin the list of divine qualities. These are qualities to cultivate, not labels for judging other people.','Do a difficult honest thing with care, even when you feel nervous.','Which quality needs practice rather than a declaration?'],
'16.2':['Strength can include gentleness','Non-injury, truthfulness, freedom from anger, compassion and gentleness are among the qualities described.','Say “That hurt me; I need us to speak differently” without inventing accusations.','Can you be truthful without trying to wound?'],
'16.21':['Recognize destructive impulses','Desire, anger and greed are named as gateways of self-destruction. The warning invites restraint and discernment.','When an urge demands immediate action, ask what acting on it will cost you and others.','What does the urge promise, and what might it cost?'],
'17.15':['Speak truth with care','Disciplined speech is described as non-agitating, truthful, pleasant and beneficial, alongside study. It is not a license for dishonesty or avoiding every hard conversation.','Before responding defensively, describe the specific behavior and the change you need, without insults.','Is it true? Is it useful? Can I say it with care?'],
'17.16':['Attend to the mind beneath the words','Serenity, gentleness, silence, self-control and purity of intention are named as mental discipline.','Before replying, notice whether you want to solve the problem or punish the person.','What is your real intention?'],
'18.63':['Reflect, then choose','After teaching, Krishna tells Arjuna to reflect fully and act as he chooses. The dialogue includes deliberation and agency.','Get advice about a career decision, examine the reasons, and own your choice instead of outsourcing it.','What choice can you explain to yourself honestly?'],
'18.66':['The teaching of surrender','Krishna’s call to take refuge in him is interpreted differently across commentarial traditions. Read it with the surrounding teaching and a trusted commentary, not as a slogan to abandon responsibility.','A devotional application is to release the demand to control everything while continuing to act with care.','Where could trust soften your need for total control?'],
'18.73':['Clarity becomes action','Arjuna says his confusion is dispelled and resolves to act. It is his response at the end of a sustained dialogue.','After reflection, write down your next step and when you will take it.','What will you do with what you now understand?']
};
const JOURNEYS = [
{id:'outcomes',name:'Fear of failure',hi:'परिणाम की चिंता',words:['fail','exam','interview','result','anxious','anxiety','worried','worry','overthink','nervous','anxiety','fear','career','भय','चिंता','परीक्षा','असफल','डर','darr','dar','future'],intro:'Begin with what is yours to do, find steadiness around the outcome, and return to one responsible action.',steps:[['2.47','Separate action from outcome','Start by identifying your responsibility without demanding a guaranteed reward.'],['2.48','Meet the result with steadiness','Once effort is clear, practice a balanced response to success and failure.'],['3.8','Choose the next action','Finish with something you can actually do, so reflection does not become avoidance.']],practice:'Write one action you can complete today and one result you will stop trying to control.'},
{id:'anger',name:'Anger & reacting',hi:'क्रोध और प्रतिक्रिया',words:['angry','anger','rage','react','defensive','argue','argument','fight','insult','गुस्सा','क्रोध','झगड़ा','gussa','gusse'],intro:'Notice the escalation, protect your judgment, then choose speech that is both honest and considerate.',steps:[['2.62','Notice what keeps feeding the feeling','First look upstream: repeated dwelling can tighten attachment and desire.'],['2.63','Understand the cost of escalation','Next see why a pause matters: anger can disturb discernment.'],['17.15','Choose a better response','Now move from restraint to a truthful, useful way of speaking.']],practice:'Draft one sentence naming the problem without an insult. Wait until you can say it calmly.'},
{id:'focus',name:'Distraction & procrastination',hi:'एकाग्रता और अभ्यास',words:['focus','distract','phone','scroll','procrast','lazy','study','discipline','habit','concentr','टाल','आलस','आदत','पढ़','ध्यान','padh','aadat'],intro:'Make the practice manageable, learn to return, and repeat without demanding perfection.',steps:[['6.17','Set a sustainable rhythm','Begin with moderation so the plan is possible in ordinary life.'],['6.26','Return when the mind wanders','Next practice the return itself rather than fighting every thought.'],['6.35','Repeat, with less clinging','End with practice and detachment, the response Krishna gives to a restless mind.']],practice:'Choose a ten-minute task. Put the distraction out of reach. When attention wanders, return to one next line.'},
{id:'relationships',name:'Care & relationships',hi:'संबंध और करुणा',words:['love','partner','relationship','girlfriend','boyfriend','friend','family','hurt','care','breakup','break up','rejection','प्यार','रिश्ता','प्रेम','दुख','dost','pyar'],intro:'Begin with compassion, express the truth carefully, and examine the intention underneath your response.',steps:[['12.13','Begin with compassion','Meet the other person as someone with feelings, without hatred or possessiveness.'],['17.15','Speak with care','Turn that attitude into truthful, beneficial words.'],['17.16','Check the intention','End by asking whether your reply seeks understanding or retaliation.']],practice:'Ask what the other person needs you to understand, then reflect it back before giving your view. Boundaries and safety still matter.'},
{id:'comparison',name:'Comparison & self-worth',hi:'तुलना और आत्मसम्मान',words:['compar','jealous','envy','worth','inferior','superior','ego','pride','confidence','self doubt','self-doubt','ईर्ष्या','तुलना','अहंकार'],intro:'Notice the comparison loop, become fairer toward yourself, and widen your respect for others.',steps:[['2.62','See the attention loop','Begin by noticing repeated dwelling on something you think will complete you.'],['6.5','Practice self-support','Then choose actions and self-talk that help you rise instead of tearing you down.'],['5.18','Practice equal regard','End by looking beyond status, appearance and rank when valuing people.']],practice:'Name one skill to practice and one person to treat with more respect today.'},
{id:'decisions',name:'A difficult decision',hi:'धर्मसंकट और निर्णय',words:['decision','choose','choice','confus','purpose','duty','right','wrong','conflict','dharma','निर्णय','कर्तव्य','उलझन','सही','galat','sahi'],intro:'Admit uncertainty, seek sound guidance, then reflect and make a considered choice.',steps:[['2.7','Be honest about uncertainty','Start where Arjuna does: acknowledge that you need help discerning your duty.'],['4.34','Ask a sincere question','Move toward learning, with humility and inquiry rather than a demand for validation.'],['18.63','Take responsibility for the choice','After reflection, the decision must become your own considered action.']],practice:'List the people affected, your responsibilities, and one question to ask a trusted person.'},
{id:'grief',name:'Loss & change',hi:'शोक और परिवर्तन',words:['grief','loss','lost','death','died','bereav','sad','lonely','change','शोक','मृत्यु','अकेला','उदास','खो'],intro:'Let the difficulty be acknowledged, make room for change, and seek a compassionate way through the day.',steps:[['2.7','Let yourself ask for support','Begin with an admission of difficulty rather than an expectation to have answers.'],['2.14','Give changing feelings room','Reflect on the changing nature of experience without demanding that grief end quickly.'],['12.13','Return to compassion','Close with a quality you can offer yourself and another person today.']],practice:'Choose one person you can contact and one small act of care. There is no required timetable for grief.'},
{id:'desire',name:'Desire & attachment',hi:'इच्छा और आसक्ति',words:['desire','tempt','craving','lust','greed','attachment','addict','urge','लालच','कामना','वासना','इच्छा','आसक्ति'],intro:'Notice attachment developing, consider its consequences, and practice restraint without self-hatred.',steps:[['2.62','Notice what strengthens the urge','Start with attention: what repeatedly brings the object back into your mind?'],['16.21','Consider where it leads','Then examine the harm of unrestrained desire, anger and greed.'],['2.64','Practice a workable boundary','Finish with disciplined engagement rather than aversion or compulsive indulgence.']],practice:'Identify a trigger, create a small boundary, and choose a different action for the next ten minutes.'}
];
